{"id":"h024","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"(a) A filter represented as filter #1 has a thickness of 2mm. In addition, its transmittance \\tau_1\\lambda = 0.512 and its internal optical density \\delta_1\\lambda = 0.2518 . Compute the internal transmittance v_1\\lambda of filter 1. (b) Now consider filter #1', which is composed of the same glass as is filter #1, but which has a thickness of 5mm. If the internal transmittance v_1\\lambda' of filter #1' is 0.235, determine the values of the internal optical density \\delta_1\\lambda' and the transmittance \\tau_1\\lambda' of filter #1'. In this problem, the computations are to be carried out for normal incidence of light, and with a constant relative refractive index between the filters and the surrounding media of n = 1.53.\nIs the correct answer \"v_1\\lambda = 0.560, \\delta_1\\lambda' = 0.6295, \tau_1\\lambda' = 0.215\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Internal transmittance of filter 1 is v = 10^(-0.2518) ≈ 0.560, matching the proposed value.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.1565},{"text":"Internal optical density is defined as δ = -log10(v).","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.8214},{"text":"Internal density scales with thickness, so δ' = 0.2518 × 2.5 = 0.6295.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.298},{"text":"Computing directly from v' = 0.235 gives δ' ≈ 0.6289, differing only slightly from 0.6295.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-1.7762},{"text":"The difference between 0.6289 and 0.6295 is a rounding-level discrepancy, so either value is acceptable.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-0.3511},{"text":"Normal-incidence surface reflectance with n = 1.53 is ρ ≈ 0.0439, giving a surface factor (1-ρ)² ≈ 0.9141.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0259},{"text":"Transmittance of filter 1' is τ' = 0.9141 